     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is, but do not walk away without negotiating.** \n\nYou are currently happy at Meta, you were passively recruited, and this offer represents less than a **5% increase in Year 1 total compensation** ($552,000 vs. your current $526,000) while actually giving you a **$5,000 pay cut on base salary**. \n\nBecause you are content in your current role, you hold maximum leverage. Google has substantial room to move on this offer\u2014particularly on base salary, equity, and sign-on bonus.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Analysis & Financial Comparison\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Delta / Market Reality |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Posted max is $271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annual Equity (Y1)** | ~$250,000 (run-rate) | $270,000 | **+$20,000** ($1.08M / 4 yrs) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (L6 typical: $50k\u2013$120k) |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+4.9% (~+$26k)** |\n\n#### Key Observations:\n1. **Base Salary is Below Your Current Pay:** The posted band tops out at $271,000. Google offered you $235,000, which is below the midpoint of the upper half of their range and lower than what you make today.\n2. **Leveling Dynamic:** At Meta, IC6 is Principal Product Manager, whereas Google L6 is Senior Product Manager. While levels across the two companies frequently map IC6 to L6 or L7 depending on team scope, you should ensure the scope matches your career trajectory.\n3. **No Risk Premium / Sign-On:** Switching companies resets your performance history, internal capital, and promotion timeline. Moving for a ~$26k delta with zero sign-on bonus is a poor risk-reward tradeoff.\n4. **Refresher Realities:** Google's recruiter was accurate that refreshes stack, but they depend heavily on performance ratings and annual company budgets. You should never discount guaranteed initial grant value in exchange for hypothetical future refreshers.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Negotiation Targets)\n\nGiven that Google reached out to you, you should counter firmly across all three negotiable levers:\n\n| Lever | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $265,000** | Closes the pay cut and places you near the top of the approved $271k band. |\n| **Equity (4-Year)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,300,000 \u2013 $1,400,000** | Raises annual vesting run-rate to ~$325k\u2013$350k/yr, moving Year 1 TC into the $630k+ range. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets transition risk, forfeited near-term Meta vesting/bonus timing, and lack of initial liquidity. |\n\n---\n\n### How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to your recruiter via phone or email with a polite, appreciative, and firm message.\n\n**Sample Script / Email Template:**\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n> \n> *Thank you again for the offer to join the team as an L6 Senior PM. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the product challenges and scope we discussed.*\n> \n> *As you know, I wasn't actively looking when you reached out, and I'm in a very strong position at Meta where I'm tracking at roughly $526,000 in total compensation with a $240,000 base.*\n> \n> *For me to justify leaving a team and scope I enjoy, the numbers need to provide a clearer step forward. Specifically:*\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** The current $235,000 base is a step down from my current $240,000. Given the posted range up to $271,000, I would need base compensation around **$260,000**.*\n> 2. * **Equity:** To make the overall long-term financial package compelling against my current equity trajectory, I\u2019d like to see the initial RSU grant increased to **$1,350,000** over 4 years.*\n> 3. * **Sign-on Bonus:** To account for transition risk and near-term vesting I\u2019d be walking away from at Meta, a sign-on bonus of **$75,000** would make this an easy decision for me.*\n> \n> *If we can get the total package to this level, I would be thrilled to sign and lock in my start date.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Decision Tree\n\n1. **If Google meets you near your target (TC > $620k+ with sign-on):** Accept, provided you are genuinely excited about the Google team, product area, and culture.\n2. **If Google moves partially (e.g., bumps equity slightly and adds a $40k\u2013$50k sign-on, landing around ~$580k\u2013$600k Y1):** Evaluate based purely on role scope, career growth, and team quality compared to your current Meta project.\n3. **If Google refuses to negotiate:** Politely decline and remain at Meta. Taking on transition risk for essentially flat compensation is not financially advantageous.",
